WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.834 --> 00:00:02.934
-Welcome back, buddy.
It's always good to see you.

00:00:02.934 --> 00:00:04.634
We have a lot to talk about.

00:00:04.634 --> 00:00:06.267
-We have a lot to talk about.
-We really do.

00:00:06.267 --> 00:00:07.334
-First of all, the beard.

00:00:07.334 --> 00:00:08.667
You're looking handsome,
can I just say?

00:00:08.667 --> 00:00:09.934
-Aw, no, stop.
-No, I mean it. It's nice.

00:00:09.934 --> 00:00:11.267
It's like, I can't grow a beard,

00:00:11.267 --> 00:00:13.200
so whenever I see somebody
do it, I'm just --

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.367
I, like, cheer from the rafters.

00:00:15.367 --> 00:00:16.734
"You go, Jimmy!"

00:00:16.734 --> 00:00:18.501
-Yes, this took me
six months to do it.

00:00:18.501 --> 00:00:20.234
-I can't even do it.
-Oh, come on, you're a stud.

00:00:20.234 --> 00:00:21.634
-How have you been? I haven't
seen you in a while, man.

00:00:21.634 --> 00:00:23.300
-I haven't seen you, buddy.
Yeah, I'm happy you're here.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.200
-Yes, yes.
-Thanks for being here.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.834
You kind of shocked the world.

00:00:26.834 --> 00:00:29.100
You shocked me, at least,
a couple weeks ago.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.033
You announced that you're
leaving "The Daily Show."

00:00:31.033 --> 00:00:32.033
-Right, yes.

00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:33.767
-And that your last show
is December 8th.

00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:34.868
-That is indeed correct.

00:00:34.868 --> 00:00:36.767
-Why?

00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:38.634
-Um. Why not?

00:00:38.634 --> 00:00:40.534
[ Laughter ]

00:00:40.534 --> 00:00:41.934
No, you know what --
you know what I think?

00:00:41.934 --> 00:00:43.400
It's funny how people ask it.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.300
Like, people ask you
this question as if

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:46.601
you're telling them bad news.

00:00:46.601 --> 00:00:47.934
-Yeah.

00:00:47.934 --> 00:00:49.367
-You know, and I can understand
that, but I think --

00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:51.567
I think everybody
has gone through a period

00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:53.367
of looking at their lives.

00:00:53.367 --> 00:00:54.667
You know, every single person.

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:56.901
We were -- we're in our
apartments for two years.

00:00:56.901 --> 00:00:58.467
You know, the world changed.

00:00:58.467 --> 00:01:00.701
We experienced collective grief.

00:01:00.701 --> 00:01:03.067
I think if you don't
look at your life

00:01:03.067 --> 00:01:05.434
and think about what you'd
like to do differently,

00:01:05.434 --> 00:01:06.834
then you haven't experienced

00:01:06.834 --> 00:01:08.367
what we've all experienced,
you know?

00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:10.868
And so I think of this
as a joyous thing, you know?

00:01:10.868 --> 00:01:12.601
I lived through a crazy time.

00:01:12.601 --> 00:01:15.000
I shared it with people who were
kind enough to share it with me.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.067
We went through something,
and what's on the other side?

00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:19.267
I don't take anything
for granted anymore.

00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:21.100
I don't believe
that life is a given.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.367
I don't assume that things
will come to me.

00:01:23.367 --> 00:01:24.901
And so, you know,
I just go, like,

00:01:24.901 --> 00:01:26.400
"This is it. This is time."

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.334
And leave -- when people say
"Why are you leaving?"

00:01:28.334 --> 00:01:29.467
'Cause imagine if it was
the other way around.

00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:30.534
I go, like, "I'm leaving,"

00:01:30.534 --> 00:01:31.634
and then people are like,
"Yeah, yeah."

00:01:31.634 --> 00:01:33.200
[ Laughter ]

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.267
-"It's about time."

00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:35.734
-Yeah, like, leave --
leave when --

00:01:35.734 --> 00:01:37.167
-Leave when people go, "Why?"

00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:38.601
-Yeah. Yeah.
-I like that.

00:01:38.601 --> 00:01:39.868
Leave when it's still good.
Leave when you're still loved.

00:01:39.868 --> 00:01:41.300
Leave when you still have
the energy to enjoy it.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.000
I still have three months
to have a ton of fun

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.000
with everybody at the show.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.434
-Yeah.
-And I'm gonna do that.

00:01:45.434 --> 00:01:47.801
-Do you have plans on what
you're going to do next, or...?

00:01:47.801 --> 00:01:49.467
-Everything. Everything.

00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:50.934
-Everything?
-Everything.

00:01:50.934 --> 00:01:52.133
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:55.601
I'm gonna get drumming lessons
from Quest.

00:01:55.601 --> 00:01:56.767
-Yeah, Quest.
-You know this.

00:01:56.767 --> 00:01:57.901
We're getting our
drumming lessons going.

00:01:57.901 --> 00:01:59.567
-Yeah, of course.
Yeah, he'll join you.

00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:01.834
-No, you know what?
I'm so excited to do everything.

00:02:01.834 --> 00:02:04.801
I didn't get to travel as much,
doing stand-up around the world.

00:02:04.801 --> 00:02:06.200
I'm excited to do that
-Yeah.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.701
-I used to go to a country and I
would be there for weeks on end.

00:02:08.701 --> 00:02:10.634
I would learn
parts of the language.

00:02:10.634 --> 00:02:11.701
I would learn about the culture.

00:02:11.701 --> 00:02:12.868
I would do a show based
around that.

00:02:12.868 --> 00:02:14.234
I'm gonna get back to
doing that.

00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:15.334
Producing, you know?

00:02:15.334 --> 00:02:17.234
Just working behind the camera.
-Yeah.

00:02:17.234 --> 00:02:19.033
-Working on different ideas,
you know?

00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:20.400
Going back home,

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.534
spending more time with family
in South Africa.

00:02:23.534 --> 00:02:25.267
Everything is what
I'm gonna be doing.

00:02:25.267 --> 00:02:27.400
-Stand-up is --
-The show is hard, Jimmy.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:28.634
We're here every day, Jimmy.

00:02:28.634 --> 00:02:29.868
-You don't need to tell me.

00:02:29.868 --> 00:02:31.400
-Do I need to tell you this,
Jimmy?

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.033
-No, you don't have to tell me.
-Why aren't you leaving, Jimmy?

00:02:33.033 --> 00:02:34.968
[ Laughter ]

00:02:34.968 --> 00:02:36.234
It's hard.

00:02:36.234 --> 00:02:38.567
-I have an announcement tonight.
-No, don't do it!

00:02:38.567 --> 00:02:40.033
[ Drumroll ]
No, they'll blame me.

00:02:40.033 --> 00:02:42.234
Don't -- You stop
playing those drums, now!

00:02:42.234 --> 00:02:45.234
[ Laughter ]

00:02:45.234 --> 00:02:47.133
-I do want to see you
do stand-up,

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:48.667
because man, oh, man,

00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:50.267
you're selling out these shows,
and I follow you on the socials

00:02:50.267 --> 00:02:51.167
and I see what you're doing
and I go --

00:02:51.167 --> 00:02:53.334
You just sold out in Toronto.

00:02:53.334 --> 00:02:54.734
-It was amazing.

00:02:54.734 --> 00:02:56.534
It was truly, truly amazing.
-Two nights in a row, right?

00:02:56.534 --> 00:03:00.367
-Yeah, two nights
at the Scotiabank Arena,

00:03:00.367 --> 00:03:02.400
and I don't take it for granted.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.767
-You're doing arenas!

00:03:03.767 --> 00:03:05.167
-I remember when I was
in Toronto,

00:03:05.167 --> 00:03:07.067
doing a 200-seat venue.
-Yeah.

00:03:07.067 --> 00:03:09.200
-People would come, I don't even
know how they knew me.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.033
Literally, you go to
someplace in the world

00:03:11.033 --> 00:03:12.300
and people go,
"I'm your biggest fan!"

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.567
This was before social media
was really a thing.

00:03:14.567 --> 00:03:15.801
I was like, "How do you know me?

00:03:15.801 --> 00:03:16.968
I'm a little creeped out,
but I appreciate this."

00:03:16.968 --> 00:03:18.767
-Yeah, of course.
-Because how do you know me?

00:03:18.767 --> 00:03:22.067
And to go from that to now
playing these giant rooms,

00:03:22.067 --> 00:03:23.467
I don't take it for granted.

00:03:23.467 --> 00:03:25.133
I'm going back to
the small rooms again,

00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:26.601
because that's where you
start the set.

00:03:26.601 --> 00:03:29.467
But it's been
a wonderful progression,

00:03:29.467 --> 00:03:30.968
to see where you start,
where you get to,

00:03:30.968 --> 00:03:32.601
and going back to that
and appreciating it.

00:03:32.601 --> 00:03:33.667
It was fun.

00:03:33.667 --> 00:03:35.701
-I remember being nervous
not being able to

00:03:35.701 --> 00:03:38.868
sell out a 200-seat comedy club.
-I'm still nervous.

00:03:38.868 --> 00:03:41.567
-They make you do radio
in the morning, too.

00:03:41.567 --> 00:03:43.501
-I am still nervous.
-Oh, please.

00:03:43.501 --> 00:03:45.067
-Radio's fun, though.
-Oh, yeah.

00:03:45.067 --> 00:03:46.667
-But not when it's like,
5:30 in the morning

00:03:46.667 --> 00:03:48.133
and you've got to be funny.
I'm like, Oh, my God.

00:03:48.133 --> 00:03:49.167
-And they've got so much energy,

00:03:49.167 --> 00:03:50.367
and they've all got
the radio voices.

00:03:50.367 --> 00:03:51.534
They're like,
"Wake up to the morning!"

00:03:51.534 --> 00:03:56.734
[ Overlapping shouting ]

00:03:56.734 --> 00:03:59.300
[ Imitating sound effects ]

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.400
[ Applause ]

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:02.801
-Call in!

00:04:02.801 --> 00:04:04.400
Call in!

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.067
-I didn't even have my coffee!

00:04:06.067 --> 00:04:08.567
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.567 --> 00:04:11.434
-You know, the "Black Panther"
sequel is coming out soon.

00:04:11.434 --> 00:04:12.701
-Yes.

00:04:12.701 --> 00:04:13.834
-And I don't know if
a lot of people know this,

00:04:13.834 --> 00:04:15.200
but you were in the original
"Black Panther."

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.100
-I don't know if I believe this.
-Yes.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.534
[ Laughter ]

00:04:18.534 --> 00:04:19.667
These are rumors
that I've been told.

00:04:19.667 --> 00:04:20.834
-No, no, this is a real thing.

00:04:20.834 --> 00:04:22.200
-i don't know,
I think people just assume,

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.067
because it's like, Wakanda,
it's from Africa,

00:04:24.067 --> 00:04:25.667
I'm from Africa.
-No, no.

00:04:25.667 --> 00:04:26.667
-And people are just like,
"You were there."

00:04:26.667 --> 00:04:28.701
I'm like, "No, I was in Africa."

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:30.267
It's similar,
but it's not the same.

00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:31.834
-It's not Wakanda, yeah.

00:04:31.834 --> 00:04:34.367
But you are
the voice of the A.I.

00:04:34.367 --> 00:04:36.300
-I'm the voice of Griot, yeah.
-Yeah, the --

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:38.834
-I'm basically, it's like
African Siri, that's what I am.

00:04:38.834 --> 00:04:41.067
[ Laughter ]

00:04:41.067 --> 00:04:42.868
That's essentially
what I'm doing.

00:04:42.868 --> 00:04:44.000
-You've made it.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:45.334 --> 00:04:48.834
Did you just tell every
single human that you knew

00:04:48.834 --> 00:04:50.601
on this planet that you were
in "Black Panther"?

00:04:50.601 --> 00:04:51.968
And you're like, "Dude,
I'm in the biggest movie ever."

00:04:51.968 --> 00:04:53.767
-No. No, you know why?
Because I hate spoilers.

00:04:53.767 --> 00:04:55.033
-Yeah.

00:04:55.033 --> 00:04:57.567
-And so I wouldn't spoil it
even for my friends,

00:04:57.567 --> 00:04:59.133
to let them know that
I'm doing a --

00:04:59.133 --> 00:05:00.801
I genuinely don't --

00:05:00.801 --> 00:05:02.100
I don't watch trailers,
for instance.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.300
-You don't?
-No, I hate trailers.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:05.601
-Because you don't want to know?

00:05:05.601 --> 00:05:07.801
-No, like, I like getting
into the movie

00:05:07.801 --> 00:05:09.601
and not knowing what's coming,
what's gonna happen,

00:05:09.601 --> 00:05:11.367
not anticipating a scene,
not thinking about --

00:05:11.367 --> 00:05:12.634
Sometimes they'll put a thing --

00:05:12.634 --> 00:05:13.634
Have you noticed they'll
put a thing in the trailer

00:05:13.634 --> 00:05:14.834
that's not in the movie?

00:05:14.834 --> 00:05:17.601
And sometimes you wait
for the whole movie --

00:05:17.601 --> 00:05:18.901
You're like,
"Where's the thing?"

00:05:18.901 --> 00:05:20.067
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:20.067 --> 00:05:21.300
-"No, on the trailer,
you did the thing."

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:22.367
-"Yeah, but the trailer --
the trailer had --"

00:05:22.367 --> 00:05:23.367
-"Where he's holding up
the baby's."

00:05:23.367 --> 00:05:24.434
-"There's a moment."

00:05:24.434 --> 00:05:25.634
-"He's wearing the baby's
coat and he's --"

00:05:25.634 --> 00:05:27.033
-Then there's no --
there's no baby's coat.

00:05:27.033 --> 00:05:28.400
So, uh, no, I --
-That'd be a good scene.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:31.167
-I enjoy the experience of
going to the movies,

00:05:31.167 --> 00:05:33.534
sitting there with my popcorn,
and not knowing

00:05:33.534 --> 00:05:35.968
what's gonna happen,
and then just enjoying it.

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:37.200
Go, you know --
-I'm gonna do that.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:38.367
I wanna do that with you.
-Go along for the ride.

00:05:38.367 --> 00:05:39.667
What's the worst
that can happen?

00:05:39.667 --> 00:05:41.200
-Let's just go
see a random movie that we --

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:42.634
neither one of us knows
exactly what's gonna happen.

00:05:42.634 --> 00:05:43.868
-I do that all the time.
You should come with me.

00:05:43.868 --> 00:05:44.901
-Really? I will come with you.

00:05:44.901 --> 00:05:46.200
I cannot wait.
-It's a lot of fun.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:47.567
-I know you probably
won't tell me now,

00:05:47.567 --> 00:05:49.000
but are you in
the new "Black Panther?"

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.767
[ Laughter ]

00:05:50.767 --> 00:05:52.434
Of course you're not
gonna tell me.

00:05:52.434 --> 00:05:54.100
[ Cheers and applause ]
Why would you tell me?

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.133
You just explained to me that
you don't like spoilers.

00:05:56.133 --> 00:05:57.501
-I'm just saying
you should watch it.

00:05:57.501 --> 00:05:59.100
-I am gonna watch it.
I'm gonna, yeah.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:00.267
I'll watch it?

00:06:00.267 --> 00:06:01.634
Do you want me to watch it
or listen to it?

00:06:01.634 --> 00:06:03.534
-You should -- Both.

00:06:03.534 --> 00:06:04.667
-Okay.

00:06:04.667 --> 00:06:05.968
-It'd be weird
if you only watch.

00:06:05.968 --> 00:06:07.100
If you just come into
movies with your headphones.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:09.067
-If I'm going for your voice,
I dunno.

00:06:09.067 --> 00:06:11.734
I wanted to ask you, because
this made me really laugh.

00:06:11.734 --> 00:06:13.501
Halloween is coming up.

00:06:13.501 --> 00:06:15.133
I don't know if you get into it
or don't get into it.

00:06:15.133 --> 00:06:16.300
-I love Halloween.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:18.200
It took me a while to get
into it, because --

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.734
for those who are from Africa,
you know what I'm talking about,

00:06:20.734 --> 00:06:23.267
Halloween is not something
we really celebrate there.

00:06:23.267 --> 00:06:27.167
Because my parents, my mom,
my grandma, everyone,

00:06:27.167 --> 00:06:28.701
they just go,
"that's witchcraft."

00:06:28.701 --> 00:06:30.501
-Ah. Well, yeah.

00:06:30.501 --> 00:06:32.133
-So if I was like, "Ah, I'm
gonna dress up as the devil,"

00:06:32.133 --> 00:06:33.934
my mom was like,
"Then I will send you to hell."

00:06:33.934 --> 00:06:35.400
[ Laughter ]

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:37.467
-Yeah, there was no --
-It's not Trick-or-Treat.

00:06:37.467 --> 00:06:39.701
-So it's something that
I got into in America.

00:06:39.701 --> 00:06:41.033
-It's a little different.
-And I'm in now.

00:06:41.033 --> 00:06:42.200
I'm in. I'm enjoying it.
-Yeah.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:43.501
This is my favorite thing,

00:06:43.501 --> 00:06:45.300
one of my favorite things
you've ever done.

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:48.133
Last year you dressed as --
you went as The Weeknd.

00:06:48.133 --> 00:06:49.400
-I did, indeed.
-Yeah.

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:52.767
And it was so convincing.
-I did, indeed. That's me.

00:06:52.767 --> 00:06:54.234
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:06:54.234 --> 00:06:55.267
That's me.

00:06:55.267 --> 00:06:57.400
-I know it's you.
-Yeah.

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:00.100
-But it was so convincing you
told me that people thought

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:01.534
that you actually were
The Weeknd.

00:07:01.534 --> 00:07:02.901
-Can I tell you?

00:07:02.901 --> 00:07:05.167
This is one of the strangest
experiences I've ever had.

00:07:05.167 --> 00:07:08.000
'Cause I know The Weeknd
and I look similar

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:09.467
from certain angles.

00:07:09.467 --> 00:07:11.634
The joke I have with him, is I
go, "I'm The Weekday," right?

00:07:11.634 --> 00:07:12.767
[ Laughter ]

00:07:12.767 --> 00:07:14.567
-Yeah.
-And so --

00:07:14.567 --> 00:07:17.834
I decided for Halloween,
I was like, "Ah, what's easy?"

00:07:17.834 --> 00:07:19.767
I was like, "This is great. I'm
just gonna go as him," right?

00:07:19.767 --> 00:07:21.067
-Of course.

00:07:21.067 --> 00:07:24.367
-And I'm walking downtown
in New York. It's Halloween.

00:07:24.367 --> 00:07:27.200
Everyone is dressed up
as other things.

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:29.701
Someone screams, "The Weeknd!"

00:07:29.701 --> 00:07:30.901
[ Laughter ]

00:07:30.901 --> 00:07:32.734
So I go, "Yeah, that's
the outfit. I get it."

00:07:32.734 --> 00:07:34.868
Then she's like, "Can I get
a selfie, The Weeknd?"

00:07:34.868 --> 00:07:38.300
And I was like, "Wait,
you think I am The Weeknd?"

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:40.200
She's like, "You're The Weeknd."
What are you doing here?"

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:41.400
Then I say,
"I'm not The Weeknd."

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:42.834
She's like, "You're lying.
I recognize you."

00:07:42.834 --> 00:07:44.267
[ Laughter ]

00:07:44.267 --> 00:07:45.601
I was like, "Wait. Why --

00:07:45.601 --> 00:07:47.968
So why would The Weeknd be
The Weeknd for Halloween?"

00:07:47.968 --> 00:07:50.634
[ Laughter ]
How does this work?

00:07:50.634 --> 00:07:54.234
-He wouldn't dress...
-"What is happening right now?"

00:07:54.234 --> 00:07:56.701
And then she got angry.
She got angry.

00:07:56.701 --> 00:07:58.000
I was like, "I'm not."

00:07:58.000 --> 00:07:59.968
She's like, "Don't lie. Stop
lying. Just take a photo.

00:07:59.968 --> 00:08:03.100
I love your, 'I can't feel my
face when I'm with you.'"

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:05.200
-Yeah.
-I'm like, "I'm not The Weeknd."

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:06.334
She was like,
"Why are you being like this?"

00:08:06.334 --> 00:08:07.334
I said,
"'Cause I'm not The Weeknd."

00:08:07.334 --> 00:08:08.434
-Yes, of course.

00:08:08.434 --> 00:08:09.734
-She's like,
"I thought you were nice."

00:08:09.734 --> 00:08:11.234
Then I'm like, "Well,
guess The Weeknd's not nice.

00:08:11.234 --> 00:08:12.334
I don't know what's
happening right now."

00:08:12.334 --> 00:08:14.234
-Now you're giving him
a bad name?

00:08:14.234 --> 00:08:15.968
-Yeah. So now I'm gonna
go as him every year.

00:08:15.968 --> 00:08:17.701
[ Laughter ]

00:08:17.701 --> 00:08:21.200
-He should go as himself
and have one night off.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:22.801
Let's talk about your
new project here,

00:08:22.801 --> 00:08:24.868
"The Turning Point."
-Yes. I'm very excited.

00:08:24.868 --> 00:08:28.567
-This is very, very interesting.
It's a bunch of documentaries.

00:08:28.567 --> 00:08:31.033
It's on MSNBC.
-Yes. Yes.

00:08:31.033 --> 00:08:32.467
-And these films look at issues

00:08:32.467 --> 00:08:35.300
like climate change,
voting rights.

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:36.701
-Right.

00:08:36.701 --> 00:08:38.868
-And how do you choose what
stories to cover with these?

00:08:38.868 --> 00:08:42.267
-Well, I'm really lucky that we
had a fantastic team

00:08:42.267 --> 00:08:44.400
that came together to
create this idea

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:48.367
for looking at the news beyond
just the moment that it happens.

00:08:48.367 --> 00:08:51.734
You know, I know America
loves the take of the day.

00:08:51.734 --> 00:08:53.801
I get that.
You know, I do a daily show.

00:08:53.801 --> 00:08:57.200
But I sometimes think
that we lose perspective

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:00.334
if we don't think about
the whys and the hows,

00:09:00.334 --> 00:09:02.400
the story behind the story.

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:05.000
How does it affect people in
a way that you didn't consider?

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:07.501
How do you even
separate yourself

00:09:07.501 --> 00:09:09.934
from the surface issue
to speak about

00:09:09.934 --> 00:09:11.334
what's actually happening?

00:09:11.334 --> 00:09:13.834
So, for instance, you know,
talking about polarization.

00:09:13.834 --> 00:09:15.067
Everyone says,
"Oh, America's polarized.

00:09:15.067 --> 00:09:16.634
Everybody thinks this.
Everybody thinks that."

00:09:16.634 --> 00:09:18.968
But, you know, for one of
the documentaries,

00:09:18.968 --> 00:09:21.000
we look at, do you ever
consider what it's like

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:23.901
to live in a country
where people are consuming

00:09:23.901 --> 00:09:26.801
two completely different
versions of what's on the news?

00:09:26.801 --> 00:09:28.934
You know, everyone goes,
"This is my reality,"

00:09:28.934 --> 00:09:30.934
and another person says,
"Well, this is my reality."

00:09:30.934 --> 00:09:32.567
What does that do to a country?

00:09:32.567 --> 00:09:35.167
And that's what we look at
in one of those.

00:09:35.167 --> 00:09:37.868
You know, I've been lucky enough
that the documentaries

00:09:37.868 --> 00:09:40.100
are produced and directed by
some of the most amazing people.

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:41.501
For instance, Leonardo DiCaprio

00:09:41.501 --> 00:09:43.067
produces one of
the documentaries

00:09:43.067 --> 00:09:44.467
that looks at climate change

00:09:44.467 --> 00:09:47.133
and how it ties
people's lives together.

00:09:47.133 --> 00:09:49.334
For instance,
the wildfires in Portugal,

00:09:49.334 --> 00:09:51.033
how people's lives
were destroyed,

00:09:51.033 --> 00:09:52.267
how they found each other.

00:09:52.267 --> 00:09:55.100
But then also how it ties
into a larger narrative

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:56.767
of how people
move around the Earth

00:09:56.767 --> 00:09:58.167
and why they move
around the Earth.

00:09:58.167 --> 00:10:00.167
-And how did we get there,
and also what are we learning,

00:10:00.167 --> 00:10:01.501
and how and what
are we changing?

00:10:01.501 --> 00:10:02.801
-Yeah. But just, without --
a lot of the time it's,

00:10:02.801 --> 00:10:04.901
everything is associated
with blame.

00:10:04.901 --> 00:10:08.434
And I think sometimes if we have
a conversation about what is,

00:10:08.434 --> 00:10:11.834
without looking at who you
want to or not want to blame,

00:10:11.834 --> 00:10:14.834
you can just have
a discussion around the why,

00:10:14.834 --> 00:10:16.501
and then you can go from there.

00:10:16.501 --> 00:10:19.501
I feel like we live
in a world where

00:10:19.501 --> 00:10:21.968
people argue about everything,
and I don't think

00:10:21.968 --> 00:10:23.734
you have to agree with
everyone on everything.

00:10:23.734 --> 00:10:25.434
You can go,
"That's my parking spot."

00:10:25.434 --> 00:10:26.734
I go, "That is my parking spot."

00:10:26.734 --> 00:10:28.200
But now we're getting to
a world where I go,

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:30.000
"That's my parking spot,"
and somebody goes,

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.067
"What parking spot?"

00:10:31.067 --> 00:10:32.634
[ Laughter ]

00:10:32.634 --> 00:10:34.868
I'm like, that's scary.
-That's scary, yeah.

00:10:34.868 --> 00:10:36.501
-Let's disagree...
-There is a parking spot.

00:10:36.501 --> 00:10:38.334
-...on the parking spot.
-That's what we can agree on.

00:10:38.334 --> 00:10:41.133
-Yeah, but let's agree that
there is one in the first place.

00:10:41.133 --> 00:10:42.534
And that's all I'm
trying to do here,

00:10:42.534 --> 00:10:44.501
is trying to tie
people worlds together

00:10:44.501 --> 00:10:46.968
to help them understand that
they may not agree on issues,

00:10:46.968 --> 00:10:49.834
but just give them a different
lens to see it through.

00:10:49.834 --> 00:10:52.234
And so that's been really joyous

00:10:52.234 --> 00:10:54.534
in just working through
new stories

00:10:54.534 --> 00:10:57.467
with a longer lead,
with a bit more patience,

00:10:57.467 --> 00:10:59.834
with a bit more time to build
a little more context.

00:10:59.834 --> 00:11:02.100
-The first one is called
"Shouting Down Midnight."

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:03.434
-Yes.
-What is that?

00:11:03.434 --> 00:11:05.801
-It's a really,
really wonderful look

00:11:05.801 --> 00:11:09.133
at how one lawmaker --
one person, in fact,

00:11:09.133 --> 00:11:12.801
can go on to create,
you know, ripples in a pond.

00:11:12.801 --> 00:11:18.367
You have Wendy Davis, who
stood up and filibustered a law

00:11:18.367 --> 00:11:20.234
that has affected
so many millions of women

00:11:20.234 --> 00:11:21.467
around this country.

00:11:21.467 --> 00:11:25.133
And she did it in her state,
in her world.

00:11:25.133 --> 00:11:28.367
And then what we try to show you
is how these ripples go on

00:11:28.367 --> 00:11:30.601
to create change
all over the country.

00:11:30.601 --> 00:11:32.901
And it becomes less about
that incident,

00:11:32.901 --> 00:11:35.767
and more about how people
can create more change

00:11:35.767 --> 00:11:37.534
than they think they can
in the moment

00:11:37.534 --> 00:11:39.300
that they dip themselves
into a pond.

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:41.567
-Yeah. I love that.
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:11:41.567 --> 00:11:42.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:11:42.734 --> 00:11:44.868
This is a look at the first film
of the series,

00:11:44.868 --> 00:11:46.934
"Shouting Down Midnight."

00:11:46.934 --> 00:11:50.234
-We are coming back.
We are coming back.

00:11:50.234 --> 00:11:53.434
-We are coming back.
We are coming back.

00:11:53.434 --> 00:11:56.234
[ Cheering ]

00:11:56.234 --> 00:11:58.067
-There were these moments
in the day

00:11:58.067 --> 00:12:01.267
when the building would
literally come alive.

00:12:01.267 --> 00:12:03.501
I could feel it under my feet.

00:12:03.501 --> 00:12:08.834
-Let her speak. Let her speak.
Let her speak. Let her speak.

00:12:08.834 --> 00:12:11.934
-Upset by the
the crowd's behavior,

00:12:11.934 --> 00:12:15.200
Lieutenant Governor Dewhurst
referred to the folks

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:18.334
who were there as an unruly mob.

00:12:18.334 --> 00:12:22.567
They've since taken that mantra
and worn it with pride.

00:12:22.567 --> 00:12:24.567
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:12:24.567 --> 00:12:27.000
-The documentary series
"The Turning Point" premieres

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:30.701
October 23rd
at 10:00 P.M. on MSNBC.

00:12:30.701 --> 00:12:33.033
Trevor, thank you so much
for being here,

00:12:33.033 --> 00:12:34.534
and we'll support
anything you ever do.

00:12:34.534 --> 00:12:35.901
-Always. Thank you.

00:12:35.901 --> 00:12:37.067
-You know, you're a good man.
-Thank you very much.

00:12:37.067 --> 00:12:38.467
-Congratulations.
The Weeknd, everybody!

00:12:38.467 --> 00:12:40.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:12:40.801 --> 00:12:43.167
We'll be right back with more
"Tonight Show," everybody.

00:12:43.167 --> 00:12:44.634
Come on, now.

00:12:44.634 --> 00:12:46.133
[ Cheers and applause ]

